  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000005",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "1. Weigh between 1.4 g and 1.6 g of copper(II) sulfate.",
    "reading_order" : 5,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000006",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "To do this you should weigh a test tube and record its mass.",
    "reading_order" : 6,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000007",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Then add the copper(II) sulfate to the test tube, reweigh and record the mass.",
    "reading_order" : 7,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000008",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The mass of the copper(II) sulfate is the difference between the two masses.",
    "reading_order" : 8,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000009",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "2. Add 4 cm<sup>3</sup> of water to the test tube using a graduated pipette.\n3. Prepare a water bath using hot water from a kettle in a 100 cm<sup>3</sup> beaker and stand the test tube in the water bath.\n4. Stir gently to dissolve the copper(II) sulfate.\n5. Remove the test tube containing copper(II) sulfate solution from the water bath.\n6. *Perform this step in the fume cupboard wearing gloves*. Add, while stirring, 2 cm<sup>3</sup> of concentrated ammonia solution to the copper(II) sulfate solution.\n7. Pour the contents of the test tube into 6 cm<sup>3</sup> of ethanol that has been pipetted into a beaker – mix well and then cool the mixture in an ice bath.\n8. Using a Bϋchner funnel and flask, filter the crystals. Wash your test tube with cold ethanol and add the washings to the Bϋchner funnel. Finally, rinse the crystals with cold ethanol.\n9. Carefully scrape the crystals off the filter paper onto a fresh piece of filter paper. Cover the crystals with a second piece of filter paper, Carefully pat the paper to dry the crystals. Note that to get the crystals completely dry, you may need to repeatedly move the crystals to dry parts of the filter paper.\n10. Once dry, measure and record the mass of crystals",
    "reading_order" : 9,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000011",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "***Buchner filtration set-up to filter crystals***",
    "reading_order" : 11,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000012",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**Exam Hint**",
    "reading_order" : 12,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
